## Generic Types

Generic functions can also be defined for multiple types as long as these types satisfy some constraints. As in Go, a set of constraints is defined by declaring a new interface type listing the types satisfying the constraint. In the following example, the type constraint `ints` is defined as an `int32` or an `int64`.

```
type ints interface {
    int32 | int64
}
```

In the example, we define two constraints `ints` and `floats`, and a function that converts a value with a type satisfying `ints` into a type satisfying `floats`.

Whenever possible, the compiler will use arguments to infer the type parameters of the function, as in the second call to `toFloats` in the example.
